        <p>Ranch water is the drink that made half of Austin realize a margarita was mostly sugar. Three ingredients, no shaker, no simple syrup, and it tastes like a cold front. Here's where it came from, how to make it right, what to pour into it, and how to scale it for a backyard full of people.</p>

        <h2>Where Ranch Water Came From</h2>
        <p>The origin story is West Texas, and the details depend on who's telling it. The common thread is ranch hands and roughnecks around Marfa and Fort Davis drinking a bottle of Topo Chico down a few inches, then topping it off with tequila and a squeeze of lime, because that's what was in the truck. Topo Chico itself comes from Monterrey, Mexico, and has been carbonated hard enough to survive a Texas afternoon since the 1890s; that aggressive fizz is a big part of why the drink works. Ranch 616 in Austin has served a version for years, and by the late 2010s the drink had gone statewide, then national, then canned.</p>

        <h2>The Classic Ranch Water Recipe</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Fill a tall glass with ice.</li>
          <li>Add <strong>2 oz blanco tequila</strong>.</li>
          <li>Add <strong>1/2 oz fresh lime juice</strong> (about half a lime).</li>
          <li><strong>Top with Topo Chico</strong>, roughly 4 to 6 oz, poured slowly down the side of the glass to keep the bubbles.</li>
          <li>Stir once, gently. Drop in the spent lime shell.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The bottle method is the purist's version: open a Topo Chico, drink or pour off the top third, pour in the tequila and lime, put your thumb over the top, and tip it once. No glass, no ice, nothing to wash.</p>

        <h2>Ratios: How to Dial It In</h2>
        <p>Ranch water's whole personality is dryness. If you find yourself adding agave syrup, you've made a skinny margarita, which is fine, but it's a different drink. Adjust these instead:</p>
        <table class="guide-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Tequila</th><th>Lime</th><th>Topo Chico</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Classic</td><td>2 oz</td><td>1/2 oz</td><td>4&ndash;6 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Lake day (lighter)</td><td>1 1/2 oz</td><td>1/2 oz</td><td>6&ndash;8 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Extra limey</td><td>2 oz</td><td>3/4 oz</td><td>4 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bottle method</td><td>2 oz</td><td>squeeze of one wedge</td><td>rest of the bottle</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Fresh lime is non-negotiable. Bottled lime juice tastes flat next to Topo Chico, and there's nothing else in the glass to hide it.</p>

        <h2>Tequila and Sotol Picks</h2>
        <h3>Blanco tequila under $30</h3>
        <p>You want a 100% agave blanco that's clean and peppery. Espolòn, Olmeca Altos Plata, and Cimarrón are all workhorses that punch well above their price. Avoid anything labeled "gold" or "mixto"; the added sugar and color fight the drink.</p>
        <h3>Step-up blancos ($30&ndash;$60)</h3>
        <p>Tequila Ocho Plata, Siete Leguas Blanco, and El Tesoro Blanco all bring more cooked agave, minerality, and citrus, which you'll actually notice in a drink this simple. Fortaleza Blanco is the splurge and sells out the moment it lands, so ask us to hold one.</p>
        <h3>Sotol: the all-Texas version</h3>
        <p>Sotol is distilled from the desert spoon plant rather than agave, and it's the traditional spirit of Chihuahua and the Big Bend. Desert Door, made in Driftwood just southwest of Austin, is the Texas sotol: grassy, a little minty, with a soft finish. Swap it one-for-one for tequila and you have a drink where every ingredient except the lime came from within a day's drive. Our <a href="/guides/texas-spirits-guide.html">Texas Spirits Guide</a> has more on local agave and sotol.</p>

