        <h2>Ready-to-Drink Canned Ranch Waters</h2>
        <p>Cans are how most of Austin drinks ranch water on the water, and the two names to know are both Texan:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Lone River Ranch Water</strong> &mdash; from Far West Texas. Technically a hard seltzer flavored with agave and lime rather than a tequila drink, which is why it's beer-and-wine legal and shows up everywhere. Light, dry, and the one most people mean when they say "ranch water" on a boat.</li>
          <li><strong>Ranch Rider Spirits</strong> &mdash; made in Austin with real tequila, lime, and sparkling water. It's a spirits-based can, so you'll find it in liquor stores like ours rather than grocery aisles, and it tastes noticeably closer to the real thing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Look for both in the walk-in beer cave at <a href="/lakeway.html">Lakeway</a> and the cooler at <a href="/east-austin.html">East Austin</a>. If you're heading out to Lake Travis, our <a href="/guides/liquor-stores-near-lake-travis.html">lake guide</a> covers what else to pack.</p>

        <h2>Ranch Water for a Crowd</h2>
        <p>Ranch water is nearly impossible to batch fully because the bubbles die. The trick is to batch the base and top each glass at the bar. For 8 drinks:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>16 oz blanco tequila</strong> (most of a 750 ml bottle; buy the whole bottle)</li>
          <li><strong>4 oz fresh lime juice</strong> (about 4 limes)</li>
          <li><strong>4 to 6 bottles of Topo Chico</strong>, cold</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Combine tequila and lime in a pitcher and chill. To serve, pour 2 1/2 oz of base over ice and top with Topo Chico. For 25 guests, triple it and figure roughly one 750 ml bottle of tequila per eight to ten drinks; our <a href="/guides/how-much-alcohol-for-a-party.html">party planning guide</a> has the full math. Put out a bowl of lime wedges and a jar of Tajín and let people finish their own.</p>

        <h2>Variations Worth Trying</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Grapefruit ranch water</strong> &mdash; use Topo Chico Twist of Grapefruit, or add 1 oz fresh grapefruit juice to the classic. Salt the rim and it's a fizzy cousin of the Paloma.</li>
          <li><strong>Jalapeño ranch water</strong> &mdash; muddle two or three slices of jalapeño in the glass before adding ice, or drop a slice in and wait five minutes. Heat builds, so start with less.</li>
          <li><strong>Sotol ranch water</strong> &mdash; Desert Door instead of tequila. Earthier and greener; try it with a cucumber slice.</li>
          <li><strong>Mezcal ranch water</strong> &mdash; a smoky version. Use a lighter joven mezcal, or split 1 oz tequila and 1 oz mezcal.</li>
          <li><strong>Salted rim</strong> &mdash; Tajín or flaky salt on the rim of the glass. Contrary to appearances, ranch water likes salt.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you'd rather shake something, our <a href="/guides/classic-cocktails-at-home.html">classic cocktails guide</a> covers the margarita and its friends.</p>

        <h2>Ranch Water FAQ</h2>
        <h3>What is ranch water made of?</h3>
        <p>Blanco tequila, fresh lime juice, and Topo Chico mineral water, typically 2 oz tequila to 1/2 oz lime, topped with Topo Chico over ice. There's no sugar or syrup in a classic ranch water.</p>
        <h3>Can I use a different sparkling water?</h3>
        <p>You can, but Topo Chico's unusually strong carbonation and mineral bite are most of the drink's character. Other mineral waters work in a pinch; soft, lightly carbonated seltzers make a noticeably flatter drink.</p>
        <h3>Does Lone River Ranch Water contain tequila?</h3>
        <p>No. Lone River is a hard seltzer flavored with agave and lime, not a tequila-based drink. Ranch Rider is the Texas canned ranch water made with real tequila, and because it's spirits-based it's sold in liquor stores rather than grocery stores.</p>
